      "summary": "The valley's long agricultural tradition links coastal dairies, South Hills cattle ranches, family farms, horse properties and youth agricultural programs.",
      "body": "Ranching and farms form a major part of [[valle|Valle de Santa Aurelia]] life, with cattle, dairying, hay, orchards, horses and mixed operations in suitable valleys and gentler South Hills and upper-valley terrain. Indigenous land stewardship predates the colonial arrival of livestock; Spanish-influenced ranching grew after 1608, later joined by multiple migrant traditions, including [[wyoming-ranchers|Wyoming ranching families]]. Most working ranches are outside protected [[witchwood|Witches' Wood]].\n\nA strong regional [[valley-4h|4-H tradition]] and [[valley-rodeo|major rodeo culture]] are defining contemporary institutions. Actual pasture acreage and specific farm families remain to be mapped. Full chapter: `docs/36-agriculture-ranching-4h-rodeo.md`.",
