      "title": "Valley 4-H and Agricultural Fair Culture",
      "category": "Culture",
      "status": "established",
      "era": "20th century–present",
      "summary": "A major youth 4-H culture supporting livestock raising, agricultural skills, mentorship and community fair traditions.",
      "body": "**4-H is a major part of valley youth culture**, involving livestock projects, cattle and dairy judging, horses, poultry, sheep, goats, food preservation, gardening, forestry, woodworking and other activities. School-based FFA programs are distinct, though they may join the proposed regional agricultural fair. Children need not own farms to participate; community mentoring, club animals and leased projects can broaden access. [[valley-rodeo|Rodeo]] and ranch horse traditions are part of the same rural social landscape, though 4-H participation is not automatically rodeo competition. The institution belongs to the twentieth century, not 1608. See [[valley-agriculture|ranching history]] and `docs/36-agriculture-ranching-4h-rodeo.md`.",
